# Copy this file to `.env.local` (git-ignored) and adjust as needed.

# Sent as the Api-User-Agent header on every Wikimedia request. This is REQUIRED in
# production, not just polite: Wikimedia's 2025 global limits give a COMPLIANT
# User-Agent (a resolvable URL + a contact email) ~200 requests/min per IP, but an
# "unidentified" one only ~10/min. Since the hosted app egresses through one shared
# IP, every user draws on that one budget, so keep this real. Put your own site URL
# + contact email. See docs/beta-readiness.md (Q3).
WIKI_USER_AGENT="Drift/1.0 (https://www.usedrift.org; you@your-domain)"
# Same etiquette for the other realms' upstreams (optional; sensible defaults exist):
# MET_USER_AGENT="Drift/1.0 (https://www.usedrift.org; you@your-domain)"

# NOTE on Gallery images: there is no proxy on/off flag any more. Artwork is ALWAYS
# served through /api/img/met/... because The Met publishes only fixed sizes (its
# largest "small" derivative is ~600px, too soft for a card) and sends no CORS
# header (which the trail map's crossOrigin thumbnails need). Both are structural,
# not optimisations, so there is nothing to configure.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Papers realm (Phase 17 — arXiv). Experimental / off by default. Set to "1" to
# show the "Papers" tab (arXiv preprints, CC0 metadata only, text-forward cards).
# Unset/absent ⇒ no Papers tab, nothing else changes. Drop the realm entirely by
# leaving this off. arXiv etiquette: an Api-User-Agent (put a real contact if you fork).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REALM_PAPERS=1
# ARXIV_USER_AGENT="Drift/1.0 (https://www.usedrift.org; you@your-domain)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Friends + sharing (Phase 10). OFF by default since 2026-07-27, by choice: the
# social surface pulled attention from the core reading loop. Nothing is deleted
# (pages, lib/social/*, the DB tables and RLS all remain), so setting this to "1"
# restores the whole feature: Friends + Inbox links, "send to a friend" on cards
# and trails, and the handle picker on the account screen.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — accounts & cloud sync (Phase 9). Optional for LOCAL dev: with these
# unset the app runs exactly as before, fully local (IndexedDB only), no sign-in.
# REQUIRED for the HOSTED app (Phase 13): when these are set the app requires an
# account (a sign-in gate) and each account's data is private to it. Create a free
# project at https://supabase.com, copy its URL + keys here. Deploy: see docs/deploy.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser-exposed (safe: per-user access is enforced by Row-Level Security).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxx
#
# Social sign-in buttons to SHOW — a comma list of providers you've enabled in
# Supabase (Auth → Providers). Only listed providers get a button, so you never
# show one that isn't wired up. Empty/unset ⇒ email + password only.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OAUTH_PROVIDERS=google        # or: google,apple
#
# Server-only — used by scripts/verify-supabase.mjs AND the server routes
# /api/account/delete + /api/email/welcome (they need it to remove the auth user /
# stamp welcomed). NEVER expose to the browser (never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix): it bypasses Row-Level Security. Also set it in Vercel (server-only).
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_xxx
# SUPABASE_EMAIL=you@example.com      # a test account, for the verify script
# SUPABASE_PASSWORD=your-test-password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email — transactional email (Phase 19). Confirm + reset are sent by Supabase via
# your Resend SMTP; the welcome (after verifying) + goodbye (after deleting) emails
# are sent by the app directly through the Resend HTTP API, so it needs a key.
# All graceful: unset ⇒ those two custom emails are skipped, nothing else breaks.
# Set RESEND_API_KEY (server-only) in Vercel too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxx
# EMAIL_FROM="Drift <noreply@usedrift.org>"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.usedrift.org   # for links + the logo in emails

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contact form (Phase 22). /api/contact sends TWO emails through Resend: a receipt
# to the person who wrote, and a notification to the mailbox below with reply_to
# set to them (so Reply in your inbox answers the sender, not yourself).
# Needs RESEND_API_KEY above. Unset ⇒ the form renders but sending fails loudly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the notification lands. Point this at an address your mail provider
# forwards to you (e.g. a Cloudflare Email Routing rule). Defaults to
# noreply@usedrift.org. A DIFFERENT address from EMAIL_FROM is safer: some
# providers quietly discard mail whose From and To are identical.
# CONTACT_INBOX=contact@usedrift.org

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile (optional anti-spam for the contact form). Free, and in
# "Invisible" widget mode a real person never sees it. UNSET ⇒ no script loads and
# the form relies on its honeypot + fill-time + per-IP throttle. SET ⇒ enforced
# server-side and FAIL-CLOSED (an unverifiable token is refused).
# Create a widget at Cloudflare dashboard → Turnstile → Add widget (mode: Invisible,
# hostname: usedrift.org). Set BOTH in Vercel; the secret is server-only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ads (Phase 21 — experimental, OFF by default). A single calm "Sponsored" card
# every N drifts, never saved to a trail. UNSET ⇒ no ads, no script, no cookies:
# byte-for-byte the ad-free app. This is the kill switch (toggling = a redeploy).
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_ENABLED=1                # "1" turns ads on; anything else = off
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_MODE=placeholder         # "placeholder" (house card, local test) | "adsense"
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_EVERY=5                  # show an ad after this many drift-scrolls (default 5)
# Real AdSense. Set the CLIENT id alone to load the AdSense loader script so Google
# can REVIEW the live site (no visible ads yet — this is the "under review" state):
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADSENSE_CLIENT=ca-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
# Then, once approved AND you've created an in-feed ad unit, add the slot and flip
# the switches to actually SHOW the in-feed ad:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_ENABLED=1
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_MODE=adsense
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADSENSE_SLOT=XXXXXXXXXX
# (public/ads.txt already carries the publisher line; enable the consent message in AdSense.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The supporter unlock (Phase 32). The free daily reading allowance, counted in
# STOPS (cards entering a trail — the number the feed already shows you).
#
# UNSET (the default) means COUNT BUT NEVER STOP. That is deliberate and it is
# where you should start: the app records every stop, so after a week you can
# look at real days and choose a number, instead of guessing one and finding out
# it sits just above the 25-stop nudge and reads as engineered to sell.
#
# Set it to a number and the limit switches on with no code change. Anything
# unusable (0, negative, a typo) falls back to "no limit" rather than to "limit
# of nothing", so a fat finger can never lock every reader out of the feed.
#
# Needs supabase/migrations/0005_phase32_supporter.sql to be run first; without
# it the meter cannot count and fails OPEN (nobody is ever stopped). See
# docs/supporter.md.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_FREE_DAILY_STOPS=50
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your Dutch VAT identification number (Phase 32). Article 3:15d(1)(f) BW says to
# publish it "insofar as" a VAT-liable activity is carried on, and selling the
# supporter unlock is one. Unset ⇒ /legal simply omits the line, which is right
# until the first euro and WRONG after it. No test can catch that, so it is on
# the checklist in docs/supporter.md instead.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_VAT_ID=NL123456789B01
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe — taking the €7 supporter payment (Phase 32). ALL server-only: none of
# these may ever get a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. Unset ⇒ the page renders and says
# buying is not available; nothing errors and reading is untouched.
#
# Use the TEST keys (sk_test_… / a test-mode price / the secret from a test-mode
# webhook endpoint) until you have run a full purchase and refund through with
# Stripe's test cards. Swap all three at once when you go live: a test key with
# a live webhook secret fails in a way that looks like a signature bug.
#
#   STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxx        # Developers → API keys
#   STRIPE_PRICE_ID=price_xxx            # the €7 Price object you create
#   STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_xxx      # shown when you add the endpoint
#
# The endpoint to register is  https://www.usedrift.org/api/billing/webhook
# subscribed to: checkout.session.completed, checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded,
# charge.refunded. Locally, `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/billing/webhook`
# prints its own whsec_ to use instead. Full walkthrough: docs/supporter.md §3.
#
# ⚠️ WHICH FILE WINS, AND WHY IT CAN COST YOU €7. Next loads `.env.local` AHEAD
# of `.env`, so a live key in `.env.local` is what `npm run dev` uses even when
# `.env` holds the test one. (`npm run verify:billing` reads `.env` directly, so
# the two can disagree without anything looking wrong.) `returnOrigin` allows
# localhost on purpose, so a dev-server checkout against a live key completes and
# takes real money — and refunding it starts the seven day cooldown on that
# account. Testing against live is a reasonable thing to do deliberately; the
# server logs a loud one-time warning whenever it happens, so check the console
# if you are unsure which account you are pointed at.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI layer (Phase 3 — not used yet). Left here so the shape is documented.
# The app works fully with these unset/false; Ollama is always optional.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ NOT IMPLEMENTED. These belong to Phase 3 (the local Ollama layer), which is
# deferred by choice — no code reads them, so setting one does nothing. They are
# kept as the intended names for whenever that phase is picked up. See CLAUDE.md
# §3 and Phase 3 in plan.md.
# AI_THREADS=false
# AI_REWRITE=false
# OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5:14b
